00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
well tom tuggenhead is uh with us now  hello to mr tuggenhealth thank you for  joining us on the programme this morning  um you've got dominic raab in front of  you on wednesday i believe your  committee what are you going to ask him  well we're going to be looking at  exactly what happened in this evacuation  and not just looking at what happened in  the last few weeks 10 days but also  the process that led up to it a little  bit and of course  most importantly what this means going  forward look there's a possibility we  may find ourselves with the biggest  hostage crisis the uk has ever seen uh  over 3 000  uh entitled people uh were said to be in  afghanistan at the beginning of this  process i don't know how many it is now  but we'll be asking about that and uh  we'll be looking to see what that means  for getting british citizens out what  that means for getting entitled people  out and protecting those people who  quite rightly are literally in fear of  their lives now  what do you make of the americans  sharing the biometrics and other  personal details of those that had  helped the allies uh over the last 20  years with the taliban  look i i find it absolutely  extraordinary um  i mean i i barely have words to say how  stunned i am that this should be  considered by anybody uh a good idea we  all  in the very basic uh security of our  lives are  increasingly careful about our own data  security because we know that somebody  could hack our bank account or you know  post things claiming to be us on the  internet or whatever  this is just a league beyond that this  is  quite literally uh giving information to  somebody who you know is trying to kill  them  um  i mean i don't know what to say it's  completely extraordinary  i'm guessing that the brits will not  have done something like that  well that's one of the questions we're  going to be asking isn't it  i would have thought so um ben wallace  said to us this morning the defense  secretary um that the operation to uh at  the hotel at the barron hotel has now  ended but it was always going to end at  half past four this morning of course he  would say that wouldn't he because he  wouldn't want um isis k or the taliban  to think that we have brought forward  our operations because of what happened  with the bombings yesterday but we must  take him at his word what it does mean  is that in the next few hours anybody  that is still trapped outside the  airport perimeter or indeed somewhere  else around  the capital of afghanistan will not be  getting out on one of those flights you  know the area well how difficult will it  be  to maybe go over a land border  well that's what i'm literally trying to  work out at the moment and what i was  doing just before we went uh before i  came to speak to you is i've been  texting  people in the region our envoys in  various different countries um people i  know  former warlords i worked with literally  anybody i can i can think of to try and  get friends of mine out uh and into  various the neighboring countries look  there are ways in and out of afghanistan  it is after all a landlocked country and  that means that you can always walk over  the border somewhere  but the reality is it's also a very very  difficult terrain and a lot of the  neighboring states quite understandably  are looking to protect themselves from  the overspill of terrorism of the kind  that we just saw in kabul  so i'm  you know i'm i'm very very concerned for  anybody who's left behind i'm sure ben  is telling uh absolutely the truth when  he says it this was the time when it was  going to end because the reality is we  were always going to have to end before  the americans left and the americans  are leaving on the 31st because we can  get that extension so i'm sure ben is  absolutely right but it still leads as  he himself said  hundreds perhaps thousands of people  behind in what could be the biggest  hostage crisis the uk has ever dealt  with what do we do with the military  hardware that is um left behind does it  get um scorched earth policy or does it  get brought back to potentially be used  again should we return to afghanistan  what happens  well the priority will be the people uh  absolutely without question and anything  we can't take will be destroyed this is  what defeat looks like i'm afraid  i don't think somehow it's sunk into to  to some people who have been asking me  questions recently  defeat means you don't control the  situations anymore defeat means you  don't have a voice in the debate defeat  means you don't get a say  we have just been defeated we have no  influence over kabul anymore  we have no influence over the behavior  of the taliban except by asking them  nicely and luring them with aid  to help people who they're quite happy  to kill so frankly what influence that  is i'm not very convinced at the moment  so i'm afraid  you're right this is awful this is  absolutely tragic and this is why i'm  working 24 7 to try and get those people  who  stood by us in our hour of need out  because it's our moment to stand by them  we can't allow this defeat to become a  wider defeat and we need to reorganize  re-engage with allies and remember that  britain does have a voice if it wants to  use it  i was speaking to an army interpreter  this morning he said that um he he and  his family would be killed by the  taliban unless he was taken out he he  pleaded uh directly to the prime  minister but he's not at the airport  he's hiding in place and and it doesn't  look as though he's going to be on these  flights uh coming out what does defeat  look like we know what it looks like for  him what does it mean look like for the  army veterans who lost limbs or the  parents of those uh  450 or so um that died uh in afghanistan  it's exceptionally painful i mean i i  have lost nothing like  what those parents or the partners of  those who've been killed in action have  lost i've you know i lost a few years  and  and some friends who meant a lot to me  but it's not the same as losing your  life partner your father your son your  daughter  it's not the same and and i'm sure it's  exceptionally painful  for all those people  and that's why we need to remember what  they fought for  and why what they fought for matters  they fought for each other first of all  they fought for the men and women on  either side of them and their courage  saved lives  they also fought for the people of  afghanistan  and their courage saw girls educated saw  infant mortality drop  saw opportunities grow saw people  transform and it saw the taliban yes  come back but come back to a very  different afghanistan  and finally they fought for the values  of the british people and they expressed  them  literally to the end  in an act of supreme courage that  frankly few of us could match and for  that we should all be eternally grateful  because we're seeing that same courage  reflected  by the soldiers in kabul today we saw it  reflected in the u.s marines who were  killed yesterday  they died  keeping their friends safe they died on  guard  making sure that the enemies of their  friends the enemies of their people  would not pass  now that is courage and that is what  these people this is what our veterans  brought to afghanistan and bring to our  country so  let's not say that they were defeated  they weren't it's a political decision  it's a decision very unwisely made in  the white house  and it's one that we will all pay for  but it is not a military defeat before i  let you go um coming up um immediately  after you i'm going to be speaking to a  former interpreter  who worked for the brits whose  application has been refused what would  you say to him either  as a former soldier or as a politician  i i have nothing to say as a former  soldier as a politician but as a person  all i can say is my heart goes out to  you  those who stood up for us  deserve the protection  that they are entitled to expect because  we promised it you know this isn't a  deal  some people have invented just now this  isn't some sort of you know after the  fact offer  we made this deal we made this promise  and the whole point about britain's  strength in the world is that our word  is our bond when we say something we  mean it that's what has built up  britain's strength over 200 300 years  when we've made threats we've carried  them out when we've made promises we've  stood by them when we've made allies  we've been there for them  we better do we keep doing that again  otherwise we're going to seriously  undermine our position okay tom tugan  huh thank you very much indeed for  joining us

